    "id": "citizens_of_bamberg",
    "title": "Bamberg’s Imperial Imprint",
    "tagline": "Power, piety, and a city made",
    "description": "To tell a story of “Citizens of Bamberg” from this registry is to tell it through the people who most visibly shaped Bamberg’s identity at its moment of greatest constitutional and spiritual consequence: the early 11th century, when Bamberg was elevated into an imperial project. In this lens, “citizenship” is less a modern legal status than a lived bond—patronage, pilgrimage, and memory—binding rulers, church institutions, and urban life.\n\nHenry II and Cunigunde of Luxembourg stand at the city’s formative crossroads: empire and ecclesiastical reform, political strategy and personal sanctity. Their legacy is not simply that they passed through Bamberg, but that they anchored it—by endowing institutions, concentrating prestige, and leaving behind a cult of remembrance that would echo through the city’s streets, churches, and civic self-understanding for centuries.\n\nThis meta-story follows Bamberg’s origin as an imperial-religious “city idea,” tracing how a medieval foundation becomes an enduring civic identity—one that later Bambergers would inherit, contest, and reinterpret, even when the founders were long gone.",
    "person_ids": [
      "henry_ii",
      "cunigunde_of_luxembourg",
      "e_t_a_hoffmann",
      "georg_wilhelm_friedrich_hegel"
    ],
